---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import births from "../data/mazza-births.json";
import acts from "../data/sicilian-acts.json";
const { households, plates } = acts;
const ros = households.find((h) => h.father.startsWith("Rosario"));
const sal = households.find((h) => h.father.startsWith("Salvatore"));
const pas = households.find((h) => h.father.startsWith("Pasquale"));
const plate = (f) => plates.find((p) => p.f === f);
---
<Base title="The Sicilian Registers" eyebrow="Read 10 September 2026"
      dek="Nine annual indexes and six acts, hunting one man. He is still missing — and in looking for him the archive found Rosario, and a generation above him nobody knew was there.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The family tree says <strong>Salvatore Mazza was born at Piedimonte Etneo in
    1879</strong>, son of a <P n="Rosario Mazza" /> about whom exactly one thing was known: that he
    existed. Finding Salvatore's act of birth was supposed to name Rosario and settle everything.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Salvatore is not in the register</strong> — not in
    1877, 1878, 1879, 1880, 1881 or 1882 at Piedimonte Etneo, nor at Mascali or Giarre in 1879.
    But the five Mazza births that <em>are</em> there, and one marriage act, have between them built
    <strong>three documented households</strong> and reached <strong>a generation the family tree
    does not contain at all</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The finding</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Venerio Mazza and Domenica Cavallaro</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">A birth act names a father. <strong>A marriage act
    names both fathers and both mothers</strong> — which is why one marriage was worth more than five
    births. On <strong>16 January 1876</strong> at seven in the evening, in the town hall of
    Piedimonte Etneo:</p>
    <div class="hero-plate">
      <img src={u("/plates/matr1876-act5-mazza-sciacca.jpg")} alt="Piedimonte Etneo marriage register 1876, act 5" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">1° <strong>Mazza Salvatore</strong>, di anni
        <strong>trentaquattro</strong>; <strong>bracciante</strong>, nato in Piedimonte Etneo,
        residente in Piedimonte Etneo, <strong>figlio di Venerio</strong>, residente in Piedimonte
        Etneo, <strong>e di Cavallaro Domenica</strong>… 2° <strong>Sciacca Concetta</strong>, di
        anni <strong>diciannove</strong>; contadina, nata in Piedimonte Etneo,
        <strong>figlia del fu Antonino</strong>… <strong>e di Greco Rosaria</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>Venerio Mazza and Domenica Cavallaro</strong> — a couple of the 1810s or 1820s, and
        the earliest Mazza this archive can name from a document. Nothing in Michael Mazza's family
        tree reaches them.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem">And it explains a name. Three years later Salvatore's first
        recorded son was christened <strong>Venerio</strong> — a rare name, and in Sicily the first
        son takes the paternal grandfather's. <strong>The naming custom and the document agree.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Three households, reconstructed from the acts</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Mazza of Piedimonte Etneo, 1877–1880</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">All of them <em>braccianti</em> — day-labourers —
    and all within a few streets of each other.</p>
    <div class="stack">
      {households.map((h) => (
        <div class="house">
          <div style="font-size:21px;line-height:1.25">
            <strong>{h.father}</strong> <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">({h.fatherBorn})</span>
            {" × "}<strong>{h.mother}</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="mono" style="margin-top:5px;color:var(--ink-3)">{h.streets.join(" · ")}</div>
          <div class="evlist" style="margin-top:12px">
            {h.children.map((c) => (
              <div><b>{c.date.split(" ").slice(-1)}</b>{c.name} — act {c.act}, {c.date}</div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:24px"><strong>Rosario is real, and he is here.</strong> Aged
    <strong>31</strong> in October 1877 and <strong>32</strong> in May 1880 — born about
    <strong>1846–48</strong> — a bracciante, married to <strong>Leonarda Bonaccorso</strong>, with
    sons <strong>Orazio</strong> and <strong>Gaetano</strong>. The archive has carried him as a name
    with a single asserted fact attached. He now has an age, a trade, a wife, two children and three
    streets.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why this matters for the hundred and ten</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The join, and what still holds it open</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The <a href={u("/components")}>second component</a> of the family tree — a hundred and ten
      Piedimonte Etneo Mazza joined to this family by nothing — is headed by a
      <strong>Rosario Mazza born 1850</strong>, and among his children the tree lists an
      <strong>Orazio</strong>.</p>
      <p>The register gives a <strong>Rosario Mazza born about 1846–48</strong>, of the same small
      town, whose son is <strong>Orazio</strong>. That is a far better correspondence than a shared
      surname — the right man, the right decade, the right town, and the right child's name.
      <strong>It is still not proof.</strong> The tree dates Orazio to 1883; the register to 1877.
      Two Rosario Mazza with two sons called Orazio in one town is unlikely, but this archive has
      already been burned once by assuming that unlikely means impossible.</p>
      <p><strong>What would close it:</strong> Rosario Mazza's own marriage act. It would give his
      parents — and if they are <em>Venerio Mazza and Domenica Cavallaro</em>, then Rosario and
      Salvatore are brothers, the whole Via Terremorte kindred is one family, and the tree's hundred
      and ten hang off a documented couple. It is <strong>not</strong> in the 1875 or 1876 marriage
      registers, both of which have been read, so it is 1874 or earlier.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What was read</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th>Comune</th><th class="m">Act</th><th>Mazza birth</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{births.map((b) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m">{b.year}</td><td>{b.comune}</td><td class="m">{b.act}</td>
            <td>{b.found ? <strong>{b.name}</strong> : <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">no Mazza in the index</span>}</td>
            <td>{b.father !== "—" ? `${b.father}, ${b.fatherAge}` : "—"}</td>
            <td>{b.mother !== "—" ? b.mother : "—"}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">Piedimonte Etneo births 1877–1882 and the 1879 supplement; Mascali 1879; Giarre
    1879; Piedimonte Etneo marriages 1875 and 1876. Archivio di Stato di Catania, Stato civile
    italiano, via Antenati. Every volume carries an alphabetical <em>Indice Annuale</em> in its last
    pages — which is how twelve volumes were checked without reading one of them through.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The acts</h2>
    <div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));margin-top:16px">
      {plates.map((p) => (
        <figure style="margin:0">
          <img src={u("/plates/" + p.f)} alt={p.t}
               style="border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:3px;display:block;width:100%" />
          <figcaption class="cap">{p.t}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Two corrections this made</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>The index is not the act.</strong> The 1877 annual index lists act 168 as
      <em>Mazza Grazia</em>. The act itself says <strong>Orazio</strong> — a boy, not a girl. An index
      is a clerk's abstract and it can be wrong; the act is the record.</p>
      <p><strong>And a misreading of my own, corrected by a second document.</strong> Reading act 205
      alone, this archive gave Salvatore Mazza's wife as <em>Greco Concetta</em> — which produced an
      absurdity, two women of that name on one street bearing children six weeks apart. The 1876
      marriage index settles it: Salvatore married <strong>Concetta Sciacca</strong>. It is
      <strong>Pasquale</strong> whose wife is Concetta Greco, and that is confirmed twice, in 1877 and
      1879. Two documents beat one, and the absurdity was the tell.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
